# =============================================================================
# public/.htaccess — the document root. plan.md §9.1, §17.2 step 1, §12.3.
# =============================================================================
# THIS FILE IS IN THE DOCROOT, NOT THE APP ROOT.
#   §17.2 step 1 sets the subdomain's docroot to `.../public`. Apache only reads
#   .htaccess files in the directory tree it is serving, so a file at the project
#   root would never be consulted. That is why the functional directives live here
#   and the project root carries a fail-safe instead.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
#   It does not set HSTS. The app emits `Strict-Transport-Security` in production
#   from its after_request hook (§12.3), and having a header defined in two places
#   means one of them is eventually wrong. The division is: Apache guarantees the
#   visitor is on HTTPS at all, the app states the policy.
#
#   It does not set CSP, X-Frame-Options or Referrer-Policy either, for the same
#   reason — those come from the app, where they are covered by tests.
#
# ⚠ NOT VERIFIED LOCALLY. This machine has no Apache and no Passenger, so these
#   directives have not been exercised. cPanel's "Setup Python App" writes its own
#   Passenger block, and reconciling the two is a deploy-time task (§17.2 step 4).
#   If Passenger's block is already present and working, keep it and take only the
#   HTTPS redirect and the dotfile rule from this file.
# =============================================================================

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
  RewriteEngine On

  # ── Force HTTPS (§17.2 step 2, §12.3) ─────────────────────────────────────
  # Done at the web-server layer so an http:// request never reaches Python at all.
  # Enforcing it only in Flask would mean a misconfigured deploy serves plaintext
  # for as long as it takes somebody to notice.
  #
  # X-Forwarded-Proto is checked as well as %{HTTPS}: behind cPanel's proxy the
  # connection from the proxy to Apache is often plain http, so %{HTTPS} alone is
  # off even for a visitor who is on https — which produces an infinite redirect
  # loop, the classic way this rule is got wrong.
  RewriteCond %{HTTPS} !=on
  RewriteCond %{HTTP:X-Forwarded-Proto} !=https
  RewriteRule ^ https://%{HTTP_HOST}%{REQUEST_URI} [R=301,L]
</IfModule>

# ── Defence in depth: nothing dot-prefixed is ever served ────────────────────
# The docroot should contain no dotfiles at all. This rule exists so that a future
# mistake — a .env copied into the wrong directory, an editor backup — is a 403
# rather than a readable file on a government domain.
<FilesMatch "^\.">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# Directory listings off. A listing of the docroot is an inventory.
Options -Indexes -MultiViews

# ── Passenger ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Uncomment and set the absolute path ONLY if cPanel's generated block is absent.
# The path cannot be guessed: it contains the cPanel account name, which §17.1
# deliberately keeps out of this repository.
#
#   PassengerAppRoot /home/<user>/sylhet.dydaiproject.com
#   PassengerAppType wsgi
#   PassengerStartupFile passenger_wsgi.py
#   PassengerBaseURI /
#   PassengerPython /home/<user>/virtualenv/sylhet.dydaiproject.com/3.12/bin/python

# ── Do not serve the app's own files as documents ────────────────────────────
# With Passenger the application lives ABOVE this directory. If a request ever
# reaches a path that looks like source, refuse it rather than letting Apache try
# to resolve it.
RedirectMatch 404 ^/(app|migrations|tools|tests|assets|design-src|instance|var)(/|$)
RedirectMatch 404 ^/\.(env|git|gitignore|gitattributes)
